  "result": "**Final verdict: `OK`** (2 of 6 gate runs used).\n\n**Body** (`library/TrafficSpec/Concepts/interaction/following.lean`):\n\n```lean\ndef aheadInLane : Formula := .atom \"rel_ahead_behind_in_lane\" [aB, aA] []\ndef headingsAligned : Formula := .atom \"rel_same_direction\" [aA, aB] [pSameDirectionTolerance]\ndef body : Formula := .and aheadInLane headingsAligned\n```\n\nThe order atom takes the formals as `(b, a)` because its contract makes the *first* argument the one ahead; the same-direction atom gets the formal parameter forwarded unchanged. No temporal operator, no literal, no narrowing — each element has one atom that states its words.\n\n**Witnesses** (`library/TrafficSpecWitness/Concepts/interaction/following.lean`): car (track 1) and van (track 2) at 20 m/s over three observations, headings ≈1.4° apart, tolerance 0.97.\n- `holds`: both in one lane, van 30 m ahead → compliant.\n- `fails`: abreast in neighbouring lanes (regions 10/11) → the order atom is false → violated.\n- `blind`: same pair, but the carriageway is labelled a `roadway` with `unobservedKinds := [.lane]`, so Core cannot compute the in-lane order and the source supplies it measured; masking `rel.ahead_behind_in_lane` blanks that measurement → unknown.\n\nThat last point was the one real fix: the first run returned `WITNESS_FAIL` (`blind … got compliant`), because masking the atom blanks only a supplied `aAheadOfB` while Core recomputes the order from any fully observed lane geometry. `holds` and `fails` supply no relation, since Core computes both atoms there.\n\n`task_result.md` is written at `/local-scratch2/sma407/scratch_core/launch/remote/pipeline/traffic-spec-agent/results/demo22-1109-0108/iter-001/code/concept__drv_interaction_following/task_result.md`, with both gate verdicts, the witness figures I chose, and why `blind` is built the way it is. The record needed no change.",
